WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.428
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.396 --> 00:00:08.113
METE ATAMEL: My
name is Mete Atamel.

00:00:08.113 --> 00:00:10.280
I am a developer advocate
at Google based in London.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.900
JAMES WARD: Hi, I'm James Ward.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.915
I'm a developer
advocate in Colorado.

00:00:13.915 --> 00:00:15.290
METE ATAMEL: First
of all, thanks

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.200
for being here so early in the
morning after the after party.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.735
If you went there,
I'm impressed.

00:00:21.735 --> 00:00:24.110
Today we're going to be talking
about multi-regional apps

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.850
on Google Cloud.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.980
So when you think about
multi-regionality,

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.210
there's actually
two aspects to that.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:33.322
The first one is running your
code in a multi-regional way.

00:00:33.322 --> 00:00:35.030
And that's what we're
going to cover here

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.740
today in this presentation.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.710
But the second aspect
is storing your data

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.360
in a multi-regional way.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.920
Unfortunately, we
won't have time

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.140
to cover that today,
so sorry about that.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.570
Because once we went
through figuring out

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.060
what it takes to run your
code in a multi-regional way,

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.550
we realized it's really
complicated, maybe much more

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:54.120
complicated than it should be.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.212
So we have just enough
time to cover that.

00:00:56.212 --> 00:00:58.670
But the good news is that we
talked to the social engineers

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.722
guys at Google who is really
interested in this topic.

00:01:01.722 --> 00:01:03.680
And they're going to come
up with some solution

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.350
papers in the next few
months on running your code

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.940
multi-regionally and storing
your data multi-regionally so

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.240
that people can get an idea
on what to do on both fronts.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.670
Because it doesn't make sense
to have your code running

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.680
multi-regionally when your
data is in a single region.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.622
So with that, first let's
talk about why we should even

00:01:23.622 --> 00:01:24.830
care about multi-regionality.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:26.747
So James, you want to
tell us more about that?

00:01:26.747 --> 00:01:28.810
JAMES WARD: Yup.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.520
So, we, and many others,
have data centers

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.210
all around the world.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:40.220
And so we have this compute
spread all over the place.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.220
And so why would
we actually want

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.560
to have all these data
centers all around the world?

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.360
So I want to give an example
of New York to Tokyo.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.880
Does anybody know how many miles
it is from New York to Tokyo?

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.700
Guesses?

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.810
6,737 miles.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.060
And I think that's
a straight line.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.860
So, that's a lot
of miles, right?

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:07.690
But we all think like, hey, our
data is traveling via light.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.880
And light is really fast.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.010
But it actually turns
out light isn't really

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.410
fast at that kind of scale.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.910
Does anybody know
how long it takes

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:20.270
light to travel from New
York to Tokyo and back?

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.330
Guesses?

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.580
It's actually 72 milliseconds.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.600
So just to get light from
New York to Tokyo and back

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.840
takes 72 milliseconds.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.620
And that may not
seem like a lot.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.560
That's only 72 milliseconds.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.160
But in a typical
web application,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.310
and the way that
TCP works, we're

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.390
actually making these back
and forth things all the time,

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:49.850
over, and over, and over,
just to load one web page.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.990
And so, this adds up and
stacks on top of each other

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.890
to create a lot of really
high latency for our users.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.880
So if our data center is in New
York, and our user is in Tokyo,

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.430
then this latency can
really add up and create

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.420
a bad experience for them.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:11.560
So, by moving the actual
service of what we're serving,

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.110
the app that we're serving, as
close as we can to the user,

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.950
we can really significantly
reduce the latency

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.440
that our users experience.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.840
So that's one big
reason for why we

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.480
may want to be multi-regional
in our application

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.837
is just to reduce latency in our
application and for our users.

00:03:29.837 --> 00:03:31.420
And then there's
also definitely comes

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.990
into play is we're communicating
across microservices.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:37.750
So if we have microservices,
and one is in New York,

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.240
and one is in Tokyo,
then those microservices

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.760
are going to have high latency
communicating with each other

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.700
as well, so not necessarily
just about the user experience.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.940
It could be service integration
experience to you and latency

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.160
around that.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.210
So then another reason
to be multi-regional

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:59.490
is just to be able to protect
ourselves against outages.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.800
A lot of times when
outages happen,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.020
they should be pretty isolated.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.660
But sometimes, they
can propagate up.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.730
And these can be
because an engineer

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.680
like me makes a
mistake, or it could

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.390
be because of some major
catastrophic act of God.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.970
And so by being able
to be multi-regional,

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:23.410
we can now avoid some of
those issues around outages.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.890
So let's talk about
how we actually

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:29.580
would build a multi-regional
architecture to handle

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.560
these scenarios
to reduce latency

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.770
and to deal with outages.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.280
So we're going to start at
what we call the zone level.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.170
So the zone level--
you can think of a zone

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:43.530
essentially as an
isolated data center that

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:49.660
is protected from other outages
or other issues in other zones.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:54.090
So it has this isolation, so
it is essentially a data center

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:58.030
with its own network, its own
power, its own backup power,

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.798
all those things together.

00:04:59.798 --> 00:05:01.590
And so we're going to
start with the zones.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.470
Our apps always will
run in a specific zone.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.630
And so we need-- that's
essentially getting down

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:07.540
to the server level.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.630
We've got to run on a
server at some point.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.150
And that server is going to
be in a data center, which

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:15.150
you can think of as a zone.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.940
Then we have this idea
of aggregating together

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.430
different zones into a region.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.060
And so if you want
your application

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.950
to be able to be
resilient across a region,

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:29.880
it needs to exist
in multiple zones

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.402
because potentially a
whole zone could go out.

00:05:32.402 --> 00:05:34.860
If there's something crazy that
happens in the data center,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.950
or somebody makes a mistake
in a network configuration,

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:40.180
or something like that,
can take out a whole zone.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.990
So if you want to be resilient
at the regional level,

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:47.080
you need to be spread
across multiple zones.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.170
And so we have many
different regions

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:50.770
that you can choose from.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:53.030
And there's US West.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:54.360
There's a couple in US West.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.130
There's Tokyo.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.230
There's Europe, all sorts
of different regions

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.910
that you can choose from.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.390
But region is just an
aggregation of multiple zones

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.000
for your application to run in.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.820
So, we've got these
multiple regions.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.450
And that's great, but we don't
want to tell users in Tokyo,

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.150
hey, you need to
go to FooTokyo.com,

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.070
and users in Europe
to say, oh you

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.398
got to go to
FooEurope.com, right?

00:06:19.398 --> 00:06:20.440
That's not going to work.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.262
We want to send them to Foo.com.

00:06:22.262 --> 00:06:24.660
And so then we have
to have some way

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:27.990
to be able to give them
a single endpoint that

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.680
then will route traffic to their
nearest region that they're in.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.450
And this is what a
global load balancer

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:36.900
does is it provides us a
single IP address, and thus

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.870
a single domain name, that
we can give to our users

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.430
or to our services.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:45.450
And it then looks at the request
and where it's coming from,

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:49.320
and we'll route it to
the closest location

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.840
and make sure that
what its routing it to

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.270
is up and able to
handle requests.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:00.560
So then our users are happy
because our users in Tokyo

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.840
automatically go to the
Tokyo region service.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.910
So great, that's how our
global load balancer works.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.720
But let's actually take a
look at what this actually

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.620
looks like.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.658
So I'm going to start here.

00:07:15.658 --> 00:07:18.200
I've got-- this is going to get
a little tricky because we're

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.040
running two different windows.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.040
Our Chrome browser on the left
is running, actually running.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:26.990
The browser itself
is actually running

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.050
on a virtual machine in Europe.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.720
And then our Chrome
browser on the right

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.200
is a virtual machine
running in Oregon.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.422
So left, Europe, right Oregon.

00:07:38.422 --> 00:07:40.880
Maybe we should've done that
the other way, geographically,

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:42.800
but that's OK.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.300
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:46.310
to load an application here.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:48.740
And you'll see the address
for this application.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.110
That's 35.244.

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:53.420
We're going to load that
same address in the other one

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:54.180
as well.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.210
But what this app
is doing is when

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:59.750
it loads, it figures out where
the application is actually

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:01.700
running, so not where
the user is coming from,

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.170
but where the application
is actually running.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.440
So this application
is running in Belgium.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.600
So it's in the Belgian region.

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.300
And then it tells us how--
the ping time that it took,

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:16.130
how long it took for the client,
which is also in Belgium,

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.670
in this case, to make
the request to the region

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:21.540
in Belgium and get a response.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:23.870
So it was pretty
quick, 49 milliseconds.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:28.190
And then it shows us a nice
picture of Belgium, of Saint

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:31.700
Ghislain, Belgium there.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:32.929
OK, so that's great.

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:34.460
But what we're
going to do now is

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.409
go to that same exact
address, but we're

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:39.080
going to do it from Oregon.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.650
And now the apps says,
hey, I'm running in Oregon.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:45.650
And we get pretty good latency
there, 121 milliseconds.

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:47.780
And we see a weird
picture of a building

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:50.580
from the dowels in Oregon.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:51.830
So that's great, same address.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.230
See, that's the key
with this whole thing

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:54.800
is we went to the same address.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.590
And that address
routed us to the region

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:01.680
that was closest to where
our request was coming from.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.480
So now we're able to get
much lower latencies.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:09.923
If I were in my Oregon virtual
machine to go directly-- so

00:09:09.923 --> 00:09:11.840
I'm not going to go
through the load balancer.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.975
I'm going to go directly
to my Europe region,

00:09:14.975 --> 00:09:18.380
and we try to load that, you'll
see that now my latency is

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:19.400
much higher.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.830
So I'm now accessing--

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:23.630
I just bypass the load balancer.

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:28.220
And now, my latency is much
higher, up to 361 milliseconds.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:31.700
So not terrible, but if we have
to stack a bunch of requests

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.830
on top of each other, this
is going to get really

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:35.250
slow really quick.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.670
So let's go back through
the load balancer here.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:39.830
And see, things are
much faster when

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:42.840
we access the region
that's closest to us.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:44.340
There's one other
thing here that we

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:48.330
can do is we can go
in and let's simulate

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:52.188
a catastrophic
disaster in Oregon.

00:09:52.188 --> 00:09:53.730
So I'm just going
to actually do that

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:57.253
by turning off all my
instances in Oregon.

00:09:57.253 --> 00:09:58.420
So we'll turn all those off.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:00.900
And it'll take a second
for them to shut down.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.300
But now, if we go
back, and now we

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:07.410
try to access from
Oregon this application--

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.890
and we'll have to wait a second
for them to actually shut down.

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:14.070
But what's going to happen
is it's going to now say,

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:17.160
I can't access the stuff
that's closest to you,

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:21.003
so I'm instead going to route
you to a server error or her.

00:10:21.003 --> 00:10:22.545
METE ATAMEL: That's
shouldn't happen.

00:10:22.545 --> 00:10:25.030
JAMES WARD: There
we go, to Belgium.

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:28.800
So it's now routing us to
Belgium instead of that service

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.450
that was down.

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:34.140
So now we get that fault
tolerance across those regions

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:34.890
just built in.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:36.432
And of course, things
are going to be

00:10:36.432 --> 00:10:40.620
much slower while that
Oregon system is down,

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:43.230
but at least it hasn't
crashed and is just

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.210
not working for users.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:47.040
So there we go.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:50.130
That's how we now have our
global load balancer that

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:55.320
is able to route stuff to the
closest application to the user

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:58.950
and handle outages across us.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.660
And let's go back
and scale that back

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:04.830
up so that future
demos work just fine.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:06.166
METE ATAMEL: Thank you.

00:11:06.166 --> 00:11:07.405
JAMES WARD: All right.

00:11:07.405 --> 00:11:09.990
METE ATAMEL: Cool,
so that was good

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:11.610
for the high-level
description of what

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:15.420
an app should look like that's
tolerant and multi-regional.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:17.790
But how do you do
this on Google Cloud?

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:19.650
Before we talk
about that, we need

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:22.037
to talk about how we can
run code on Google Cloud

00:11:22.037 --> 00:11:23.620
because it's a little
bit complicated.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:25.060
There's a lot of options.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:27.600
So let's go through those
options first, one by one,

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.120
look at the options that you
have when you run your code,

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:32.280
and then take a
quick look of how

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:35.250
to turn that code running
in a single region

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:37.460
and make it multi-regional.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.750
Now, let's take a look at this
from low level to high level.

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:44.733
At the lowest level in Google
Cloud, there's compute engines.

00:11:44.733 --> 00:11:46.400
These are basically
the virtual machines

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:51.975
running Windows or Linux
instances in a single region.

00:11:51.975 --> 00:11:53.350
You will basically
choose the CPU

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:56.260
you want, the memory you
want, the storage you want,

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:58.928
and the VM is ready
in 30 seconds.

00:11:58.928 --> 00:12:01.470
And then once you have it, you
can install whatever you want.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.490
It's yours.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.880
It's not maintained, so you
have to take care of software

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:06.590
updates and everything.

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:08.030
But it's yours to keep.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:11.240
And it's mostly for
applications that you already

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:12.267
have running in a VM.

00:12:12.267 --> 00:12:13.850
You want to move
them to Google Cloud,

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:16.970
you would mostly
use Compute Engine.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:19.393
Then if you make the switch
to containers, from VM

00:12:19.393 --> 00:12:20.810
to containers--
you probably heard

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.060
about Kubernetes, which
is an open-source project

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:24.770
to manage containers.

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:26.630
And you also probably
heard about Istio,

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:28.310
which is also an
open-source project

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:31.610
to manage the traffic
to your containers.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:35.810
In Google Cloud, with a
single G Cloud command,

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:38.863
you can get a Kubernetes
cluster with Istio enabled.

00:12:38.863 --> 00:12:40.280
So with just a
single command, you

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:42.382
have a cluster running
with Kubernetes and Istio.

00:12:42.382 --> 00:12:44.840
And that's where you would run
your continuous applications

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:46.190
with full flexibility.

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:48.710
So whatever you want to
do with your containers,

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:51.320
scale them, connect
them, Kubernetes

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:53.210
gives you all of
that flexibility.

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:54.650
And Kubernetes
Engine is the place

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:56.630
to run that on Google Cloud.

00:12:56.630 --> 00:13:00.530
Now with flexibility
comes a lot of complexity

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:03.890
because Kubernetes has its
own vocabulary, like pods,

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:06.830
and deployments, and services,
that you have to learn.

00:13:06.830 --> 00:13:09.860
So there can be a
little complexity there.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:13.290
We introduced something called
Cloud Run a couple days ago,

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:15.890
which is a really easy
way of running containers

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:17.660
in a serverless way.

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:21.020
So in Cloud Run, you basically
point to your container,

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:23.180
and then you just say,
deploy this container

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:25.670
and scale it for me.

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:27.630
And what Google Cloud
does is that it basically

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.560
deploys the container, routes
to traffic to the container,

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:32.230
takes care of
scaling down to zero

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:34.970
when no one uses it,
scales it up to whatever

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:36.410
you set automatically.

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:39.470
So all those details of running
the container and scaling up

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.165
and down is taken
care for you, and you

00:13:41.165 --> 00:13:43.040
don't have to deal with
the low-level details

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:44.123
like Kubernetes and Istio.

00:13:44.123 --> 00:13:47.720
Everything is abstracted
away from you.

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:49.970
But maybe you don't even
want to care about containers

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:53.063
because containers, even though
they're nice abstractions,

00:13:53.063 --> 00:13:55.230
you still need to worry
about writing a Docker file.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.615
And you need to worry
about your dependencies

00:13:57.615 --> 00:13:59.990
and make sure that your Docker
file is actually optimized

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:03.080
so it doesn't take too
long to load your content

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:05.180
and things like that.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.020
So you maybe as an
application developer,

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.680
you want to deal with
source code and not

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.010
the infrastructure.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.070
And for that,
there's App Engine.

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:15.410
App Engine has been
around for a long time.

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:19.550
And it's kind of serverless
before serverless was popular.

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:21.710
In App Engine, you write
your code, deploy it,

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:24.110
and you still get
serverless features,

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:26.780
like scaling down to zero
and scaling up easily.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.870
But you also get traffic
splitting, versioning,

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:31.880
and other features like
dashboards and things

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:33.740
like that for free.

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:36.190
And finally, maybe you don't
even care about applications.

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:38.680
You just want to write some
piece of code in a function,

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:41.050
define the input and
output, and define

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:44.778
what triggers that function
and let Google manage the rest.

00:14:44.778 --> 00:14:46.320
So for that, there's
Cloud Functions.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:51.333
So that's for source-based,
event-driven applications.

00:14:51.333 --> 00:14:52.750
So this is the
stack that you have

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:54.520
when it comes to running
code on Google Cloud.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:55.870
And it's up to you
basically where

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:58.370
you want to be in the stack,
the low level at Compute Engine

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:01.240
or at the highest level
at Cloud Functions.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:04.160
But the key thing is that
all of these options,

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:05.830
they are basically--

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:08.570
they're always running
in a zone in a region.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:11.560
So they're not really
designed to be multi-regional

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:14.380
by default. They're not
even designed to be regional

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:17.230
by default. They're designed
to run in a single spot--

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:19.300
a single zone in a region.

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:23.808
So you have to do work yourself
to make it multi-regional.

00:15:23.808 --> 00:15:25.850
And at the high level,
this is how it looks like.

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:30.550
So for Compute Engine, there's
Google's Global HTTP, HTTPS

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:31.263
load balancer.

00:15:31.263 --> 00:15:32.930
So there's really
good support for that.

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:34.388
So you would
basically set that up.

00:15:34.388 --> 00:15:37.840
So you would deploy two VMs
in two different regions,

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:41.410
and you would set up the load
balancer in front of them.

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:44.670
And there's good
support for that.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:46.880
Now, if you're on Kubernetes
Engine and Istio land,

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:48.690
things are a little
bit more complicated.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:52.430
So there's-- so if you're
running Kubernetes on Google

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:55.690
Cloud, or on-prem,
or some other place,

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:58.730
there's Kubernetes Federation,
and there's two versions

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:01.070
of that that we'll talk about.

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:03.080
There is a tool
called kubemci that

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:06.660
helps you to set up an
HTTPS load balancer in front

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:09.090
of your Kubernetes clusters
that we'll talk about as well.

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:11.140
But it's a temporary solution.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:13.760
There is something called
multi-cluster ingress with GKE

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:16.530
that we announced last NEXT,
but it's not ready yet,

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:19.370
so we cannot use it today.

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:22.430
And finally, if you are
using Istio for your traffic

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:25.790
management, there is a way
to configure Istio so that it

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:27.200
spans multiple clusters.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:28.290
And that's also an option.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:29.750
So this story is complicated.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:31.542
And hopefully we'll
get to the bottom of it

00:16:31.542 --> 00:16:33.670
at the end of this presentation.

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:36.170
And then for Cloud Run, and App
Engine, and Cloud Functions,

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:38.090
unfortunately Google
Cloud doesn't support

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:39.932
any native solution today.

00:16:39.932 --> 00:16:41.390
So what that means
is that you need

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:44.030
to deploy them yourself
in multiple regions,

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:47.000
and you need to figure out
a load balancer in front

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:50.150
of them yourself, something
like Apigee, or Cloudflare,

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:51.808
or NGINX, or
something like that.

00:16:51.808 --> 00:16:53.600
But we'll talk about
all of these in detail

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:56.060
as we go through
the presentation.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:58.334
Now first let's talk
about Compute Engine.

00:16:58.334 --> 00:17:03.850
Now, if you want to deploy
code to Compute Engine, one

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:07.300
wrong way of doing that
is that you get a VM,

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.819
you install whatever
you want on the VM,

00:17:09.819 --> 00:17:11.950
and you repeat the process.

00:17:11.950 --> 00:17:14.088
This works for maybe five VMs.

00:17:14.088 --> 00:17:15.880
But if you have to
manage thousands of VMs,

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:16.560
it doesn't work.

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:17.490
It doesn't scale.

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:19.630
And it's error prone.

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:21.349
What you need to do
is, first of all,

00:17:21.349 --> 00:17:23.420
you need to create what's
called an instance template.

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:24.878
And from the instance
template, you

00:17:24.878 --> 00:17:27.910
need to create an instance
group to manage those VMs.

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:29.560
But to get to the
instance template,

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:31.070
you need to do a
little bit of work.

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:33.570
So let's go through that list,
see how to create an instance

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:35.150
template first.

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:37.240
First you start
with a base image

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:38.980
Google Cloud has a
lot of base images

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:42.040
that you can rely on
for Windows and Linux.

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:43.780
Or you can also
input your own image

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:46.000
if you want into your
project and use that as well.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:47.710
By either way, you
start with an image,

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:50.500
and you get a VM running.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:52.900
From the VM, you can
install other things

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:54.190
that you need on that VM.

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:57.070
And once it's ready, you can
take a snapshot of that VM.

00:17:57.070 --> 00:18:00.580
So the snapshot basically
takes a snapshot of the disk

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:02.950
that the VM is attached to.

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:05.900
And then you can use that
disk to create other VMs.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:08.290
But the snapshot
is project-scoped,

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:11.320
meaning you can only use
it within the same project.

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:12.890
You usually have
multiple projects,

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:14.807
and you want to be able
to share this snapshot

00:18:14.807 --> 00:18:16.040
with multiple projects.

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:18.630
So for that, you create
something called custom image.

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:21.160
So custom image is
basically a snapshot so

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:25.180
that you can share globally
with multiple projects.

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:27.020
But you don't usually
take the custom image

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:29.890
and then run it as it is
because different regions

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:33.110
and different apps need
different configuration.

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:36.250
So what you usually do is
that you take a custom image,

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.210
and you use startup
scripts, and use it

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:42.220
you use metadata service to
get configuration and setup

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:44.170
into your custom
images so that they

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:47.410
are customized for the
region that you are running.

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:49.570
And from then, you can
create an instance template.

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:52.210
So that's the journey
to instance template.

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:54.540
So instance template
basically combines

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:56.620
a custom image, and
the startup scripts,

00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:58.570
and the metadata
together into a template

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:02.360
that you can use to create
groups or instances.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:07.360
And finally, you can use Cloud
API to create these instance

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:08.090
groups.

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:11.710
So the Cloud API, you can use it
from G Cloud command line tool.

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:15.460
So when you use G Cloud command
line tool, it uses the API,

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:16.930
or you can use the
client libraries

00:19:16.930 --> 00:19:20.290
in any language you want, or you
can use the REST API directly

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:20.800
if you want.

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:22.883
But either way, you're
going through the Cloud API

00:19:22.883 --> 00:19:24.280
to create these groups.

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:26.200
So from the instance
template, you

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:28.330
create something
called instance groups.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:32.830
These are basically groups of
VMs that are managed for you.

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:36.340
They are based on a template,
on an instance template.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:38.560
And these instance
groups, basically

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:40.060
once you create
an instance group,

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:41.560
it manages the VMs for you.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:44.390
So if a VM goes down,
or it's not healthy,

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:46.760
it will bring it down and
create a new one for you.

00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:49.450
You can also set auto-scaling
on these instances.

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:50.950
So you can set, for
example, the min

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:53.470
and the max size
of these instances,

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:55.510
as you've seen in James' demo.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:58.240
You can easily scale down to
zero manually or scale up,

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:00.970
or you can also
set auto-scaling.

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:03.580
But the key thing is that
although this is still

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:06.730
single zone or multiple
zones, but they're

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:11.050
still within the same region,
so it's not multi-regional yet.

00:20:11.050 --> 00:20:13.310
And as I mentioned, you can
hook up and auto-scale it

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:14.580
to your instance group.

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:17.300
And you can set
policies on that.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:21.770
So you can say, for example, I
want to scale from zero to 20,

00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:22.810
and I want to be--

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:24.670
I want to do this with CPU.

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:26.650
So the autoscaler will
look at the average CPU,

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:28.842
and it will scale up and down.

00:20:28.842 --> 00:20:30.550
It will basically
instruct interest group

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:34.320
to scale up and down depending
on the policy that you have.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:36.223
But this is still single region.

00:20:36.223 --> 00:20:38.640
So you might be wondering, why
are you showing me all this

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:39.810
because it's single region?

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:41.850
And James will
tell us about that.

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:42.930
JAMES WARD: Yeah.

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:45.330
So when we create our instance
groups in our instance

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:47.370
templates, that's
in a single region,

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:50.380
so we need to be able to take
that to multiple regions,

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:52.590
and then put the load
balancer in front of that.

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:55.770
So the solution is to put
the load balancer in front

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:57.430
of our multiple regions.

00:20:57.430 --> 00:20:59.610
So we need to set up the
instance templates, which

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:00.780
can span multiple regions.

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:03.430
But then we need to
run our instance group.

00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:07.620
So we'll run an instance group
in East or West in Europe.

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:10.290
So we'll run our instance
groups every region that we

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:12.415
want to provide service in.

00:21:12.415 --> 00:21:14.790
And then we put the global
load balancer, the Google load

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:16.840
balancer in front of that.

00:21:16.840 --> 00:21:19.230
So the way that we
do that is we first

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:21.180
need our global IP address.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:25.200
So we go and reserve a global IP
address for our load balancer.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.690
So our load balancer will
have a single reserved

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:31.600
IP address that we use
across the whole world.

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:33.670
And then we set up
a forwarding rule.

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:37.110
So the request is going to come
into the load balancer, which

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.760
is a machine running in
a data center somewhere.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:42.520
And then it needs to know
what to do with that request.

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:44.790
And so we tell it, hey,
here's what you do,

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:48.840
you need to actually call
this thing over here.

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:50.790
And so that goes
through a proxy.

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:52.920
So we set up a proxy,
which is going to allow

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.780
us to do some URL maps.

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:57.060
And ultimately,
the proxy is going

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:59.550
to point to our backend
service, which is

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:01.990
running in a specific region.

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:04.740
So we're going to tell our load
balancer about all the regions

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:07.070
that our application
is up and running in,

00:22:07.070 --> 00:22:10.620
and define those all as backend
services underneath the load

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:12.000
balancer.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:16.020
And then those actually
can forward the request

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:18.630
to our app that's
actually running

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:21.180
within a region and
then within a zone.

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:23.320
So those are actual backends.

00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:25.960
So the path that this goes
through on the internet

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:28.980
is that the end user is
going to make a request,

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:31.000
ultimately to an IP address.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.270
That IP address is going to
resolve to the region that

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:34.848
is closest to them.

00:22:34.848 --> 00:22:37.140
I think we use like multi-homing
or something like that

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:41.680
to magic tricks with multi-homed
IP addresses to make that work.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:44.710
But that IP address routes them
to the closest region where

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:46.530
there's a machine,
the load balancer,

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:49.020
running that's going
to handle that request,

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:51.120
look up the information
for where to forward,

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:55.290
where it can forward that
requests to, and then

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:57.690
try to get that request
forwarded to the region that

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:00.840
is closest to the user.

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:02.590
And then we can set
up-- we have to set up

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:05.840
firewall rules, which
allow the load balancer

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:09.110
to talk to our instance group.

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:12.680
So we need to make
that request possible.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:16.130
We in our set up just expose
through the firewall rules

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:19.880
access to the world to
our backend services.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:21.770
And you probably
wouldn't want to do that.

00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:23.780
You'd probably want to restrict
it so that only the load

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:25.310
balancer can actually
make requests

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:28.160
to your actual backend
instance groups.

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:30.392
So you set up your
firewall rules,

00:23:30.392 --> 00:23:31.850
and then you set
up a health check.

00:23:31.850 --> 00:23:35.720
So the health check is a URL
that the load balancer is going

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:37.610
to be periodically calling.

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:39.920
And then that's what
tells the load balancer

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:43.730
that that region is good
and can handle requests.

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:45.480
So that's how we set
up the load balancer.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:48.830
But let's see what it
actually looks like here.

00:23:48.830 --> 00:23:52.760
So in the Google Cloud
Platform console,

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:55.250
first we're going to
go check out our--

00:23:55.250 --> 00:23:57.922
let's go to our
instance templates.

00:23:57.922 --> 00:24:00.380
So you'll see that we have a
couple of different ones here.

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:04.310
But the one that we showed
you already was this one.

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:06.230
And so this was where
we went and created

00:24:06.230 --> 00:24:08.210
the template of how
to create instance

00:24:08.210 --> 00:24:11.280
groups in the multiple regions.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:16.160
So, there's a few things that
we have specified in here.

00:24:16.160 --> 00:24:19.100
There is the firewall
configuration

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:22.760
that is specified through
this network tag, HTTP tag.

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:25.280
There's the container image
that we're pulling down

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:30.660
from the container
repository image information,

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:33.840
so what size CPU do you
use, and that sort of thing.

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:36.230
So that's our instance template.

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:39.500
And then from there, we
created our instance groups.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:42.440
So there's the instance
group in Europe

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:45.230
that we're using and the
instance group in Oregon

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:46.620
that we were using.

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:49.140
And so if we go into one
of these instance groups,

00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:51.680
we can see this is
really just created--

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:53.960
instances created
from a template

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:55.820
and potentially auto-scaled.

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:57.780
So you'll see it's
pointing to that instance.

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:00.270
Auto-scaling is off in our case.

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:03.980
And then we have the actual
instances, the two instances,

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:07.260
that are up and running
the application there.

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:10.790
So those are actual
underlying instances for this.

00:25:10.790 --> 00:25:13.220
So that's the structure,
instance template,

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:14.720
which allows us
to create instance

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:17.210
groups in multiple
regions, and then

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:22.260
the actual running instances
that are running within zones.

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:25.880
So now let's take a look at the
load balancer configuration.

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:28.700
So that's under Network
Services, Load Balancing.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:31.480
And the load balancer that we've
set up that you've just seen

00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:33.750
is called Web Map.

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:36.428
And here you'll see
our public IP address.

00:25:36.428 --> 00:25:37.970
So that was the one,
if you remember,

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:42.110
that I loaded at first
in those browser windows.

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:44.330
So that's our public
load balancer IP

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:46.430
address, the single
IP address that we

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:50.330
use to access everything
behind the load balancer.

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:52.310
And then you'll see
that I am giving it

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:57.530
the instance groups, the Europe
and Oregon instance groups.

00:25:57.530 --> 00:26:00.950
And then it knows how to then
route the traffic as you saw.

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:03.530
And then we can go check
out our health check here

00:26:03.530 --> 00:26:04.830
to see what that's doing.

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:07.970
That's going to our
URL/ping and some parameters

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:10.110
around that health check.

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:12.740
So that's how we put
together the load balancer

00:26:12.740 --> 00:26:14.960
along with the instance
templates and instance groups

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:17.820
to make this not just
a regional application,

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:19.730
but now a multi-regional
application.

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:20.660
METE ATAMEL: Great.

00:26:20.660 --> 00:26:24.020
And the key thing is that
there's all native support

00:26:24.020 --> 00:26:28.220
here from Google, so we didn't
have to use anything external.

00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:30.680
And you get the geo
load balancing for free.

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:33.053
The global load
balancer figures out

00:26:33.053 --> 00:26:35.720
where the user is and routes the
request to the nearest instance

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:36.220
group.

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:38.410
So that's the key thing.

00:26:38.410 --> 00:26:40.122
Now for Kubernetes
Engine and Istio,

00:26:40.122 --> 00:26:41.830
things are a little
bit more complicated,

00:26:41.830 --> 00:26:44.300
so let's go through that.

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:46.050
So the landscape is that--

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:47.470
so if you are
running Kubernetes,

00:26:47.470 --> 00:26:50.500
there used to be a feature
called Kubernetes Federation

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.360
version 1.

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:56.380
There was a group that worked on
that for a year or so, I think,

00:26:56.380 --> 00:26:59.010
and then it was abandoned
because it didn't--

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:02.950
it didn't handle use cases,
and it wasn't stable,

00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:04.400
and all that kind of stuff.

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:06.055
And now there's a V2 coming up.

00:27:06.055 --> 00:27:07.930
There's another group
that's working on that.

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:10.490
And the last time I checked,
it's still evolving.

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:13.660
I haven't seen-- I think it's
either alpha or beta stage.

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:16.000
But I haven't seen it being
used in production yet.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:19.540
But if you are using Kubernetes
inside or outside Google Cloud,

00:27:19.540 --> 00:27:21.820
you should keep an eye
on Kubernetes Federation.

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:25.300
V2 will get to the final
stage, and maybe it will be--

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:26.523
you will be able to use it.

00:27:26.523 --> 00:27:28.690
But as of today, I didn't
feel like it was something

00:27:28.690 --> 00:27:30.880
that you can use in production.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:34.280
Now in GKE, in Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:38.110
the clusters are actually
deployed in a single zone

00:27:38.110 --> 00:27:40.390
in a single region.

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:43.630
There is something called
regional GKE clusters,

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:46.360
meaning you can have
a GKE cluster spanning

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:47.680
multiple zones.

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:50.620
But those are still
in a single region.

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:54.040
But one thing that
you can do is that you

00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:57.410
can create two clusters in
two separate regions yourself.

00:27:57.410 --> 00:28:00.160
And you can load balance
between them if you want.

00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:02.050
This works, but it's manual.

00:28:02.050 --> 00:28:04.480
And you need to-- basically,
it's two independent clusters

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:06.940
that you need to manage, and
you need to load balance--

00:28:06.940 --> 00:28:09.700
set up some kind of
load balancer yourself.

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:13.065
Now, there's a tool called
Multi-Cluster Ingress

00:28:13.065 --> 00:28:14.440
and there's the
command line tool

00:28:14.440 --> 00:28:17.530
called kubemci
that's open source,

00:28:17.530 --> 00:28:19.430
and it's supported by Google.

00:28:19.430 --> 00:28:24.430
And if you look at Google GKE's
documentation page and kubemci,

00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:28.330
right at the top, there's
a red font message that

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:30.280
says "This is only temporary.

00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:32.100
Don't use it."

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:35.740
But I'm going to
show it here today

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:38.128
because of unfortunate reasons
that I'm going explain.

00:28:38.128 --> 00:28:39.670
But basically, that's
a tool that you

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:43.030
can use to set up two
clusters in two regions

00:28:43.030 --> 00:28:47.050
and load balance them using
Google's HTTPS load balancer.

00:28:47.050 --> 00:28:49.930
Kind of like Compute Engine,
but it automates that for you

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:52.210
so you don't have to do
all those steps yourself.

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:55.160
kubemci does it for you.

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:57.597
So you might wonder, OK, if
kubemci is temporary, then

00:28:57.597 --> 00:28:58.555
what am I going to use?

00:28:58.555 --> 00:29:02.470
And ultimately, you'll be using
something called Multi-Cluster

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:04.370
Ingress Controller, or MCI.

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:07.930
They were announced
last NEXT in July.

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:10.236
But last time I checked,
it's still in progress.

00:29:10.236 --> 00:29:15.400
And I asked PM can we have a
version that we can play with?

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:17.380
And I couldn't get one yet.

00:29:17.380 --> 00:29:18.550
So it's in progress.

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:21.040
But eventually that
will replace kubemci.

00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:22.780
And maybe next
year, I'll show you

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:24.135
a demo of that, but not today.

00:29:24.135 --> 00:29:25.635
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:29:25.635 --> 00:29:26.860
to talk about kubemci today.

00:29:26.860 --> 00:29:28.610
But keep in mind that
it's going to-- it's

00:29:28.610 --> 00:29:31.270
a temporary solution
until the other MCI,

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:33.970
which is implemented using
ingress control rule,

00:29:33.970 --> 00:29:35.870
comes along.

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:38.320
Now, kubemci, it's
a command line tool

00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:41.830
that you can use to load balance
within two separate clusters

00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:43.960
deployed in multiple regions.

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:45.880
But there are some restrictions.

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:49.160
Each service must be configured
exactly the same way,

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:51.550
meaning that you need
to have the same name,

00:29:51.550 --> 00:29:53.420
they need to be in the
same namespace names,

00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:55.960
and they need to
expose the same ports.

00:29:55.960 --> 00:30:01.030
And they need to be the service
type NodePort in Kubernetes.

00:30:01.030 --> 00:30:02.770
So this might be a
limiting for you,

00:30:02.770 --> 00:30:07.230
but you have to basically do
this in order to use this tool.

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:09.880
And the way to do it, I'll just
show you some G Cloud command

00:30:09.880 --> 00:30:12.693
line tools quickly so you have
an idea how to set this up.

00:30:12.693 --> 00:30:14.360
First, you need to
create your clusters.

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:16.777
So in Google Cloud, you just
use G Cloud container cluster

00:30:16.777 --> 00:30:19.360
Create, give it a name,
defined the number of nodes,

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:20.300
define the zone.

00:30:20.300 --> 00:30:23.290
So this creates a
cluster in Europe,

00:30:23.290 --> 00:30:24.700
then you do the same for US.

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:26.750
So you have two clusters
running, one in Europe,

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:27.820
one in the US.

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:30.900
Then to get your
code into Kubernetes,

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:32.360
you create a deployment.

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:35.560
So you create a deployment yaml
file with the your container

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:37.000
image and stuff like that.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:38.390
And you have your code running.

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:41.220
Then you need to expose your
pod to the outside world.

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:42.970
So for that, we're
going to use a NodePort

00:30:42.970 --> 00:30:47.030
service because that's what
kubemci forces us to do.

00:30:47.030 --> 00:30:48.830
So we do that as well.

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:52.870
And then you need to let
kubemci manage your cluster.

00:30:52.870 --> 00:30:54.370
And to do that,
you basically get

00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:58.195
the credentials of your cluster
and save to a KubeConfig file.

00:30:58.195 --> 00:31:00.700
So I'm getting the
credentials of my Europe West

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:02.650
and the credentials
on my US West.

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:05.020
Save to a file.

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:10.250
And then finally, I need a
static IP for my ingress.

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:13.000
So I reserve that static
IP on Google Cloud.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:14.950
And then I point--

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:18.640
I create an ingress
resource for my kubemci.

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:20.207
So I basically
create a yaml file

00:31:20.207 --> 00:31:21.790
that defines my
ingress that I'm going

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:23.330
to show in the next slide.

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:27.000
And I point to my project,
and I point to my KubeConfig--

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:30.670
the configuration
for my MCI config.

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:35.500
So once you do that, the kubemci
can list the ingress IP for you

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:36.340
if you want.

00:31:36.340 --> 00:31:37.390
You can see the status.

00:31:37.390 --> 00:31:40.270
And then you get an IP,
and you can curl that IP,

00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:42.070
and get your cluster.

00:31:42.070 --> 00:31:43.440
So these are the steps.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:44.357
And just to show you--

00:31:44.357 --> 00:31:46.690
I am not going to show you
the deployment or the service

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:48.910
because these are Kubernetes
things that you probably

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:51.250
know about already if you
are running Kubernetes.

00:31:51.250 --> 00:31:53.380
But the yaml file
for the ingress kind

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:55.000
of looks like this.

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:57.410
You basically say, this
is my service name,

00:31:57.410 --> 00:32:00.950
this is the port that I want
to expose my external load

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:03.760
balancer basically, and then
you add a couple of annotations.

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:06.260
So you're saying this
is my static IP name.

00:32:06.260 --> 00:32:09.730
So this is the IP
name that I had

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:12.340
when I created my static
IP in Google Cloud,

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:14.770
and also did an ingress
class that tells me

00:32:14.770 --> 00:32:16.060
this is a multi-cluster setup.

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:20.020
So once you have this, you
can expose these two clusters

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:24.130
in two separate regions
using ingress with kubemci.

00:32:24.130 --> 00:32:26.107
And yeah, let me show you this.

00:32:26.107 --> 00:32:27.940
It's not going to be
too different than what

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:29.420
we had before.

00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:33.760
But if I come back here, instead
of going to Google Compute

00:32:33.760 --> 00:32:37.185
Engine instance, I'm
going to GKE instance.

00:32:37.185 --> 00:32:38.560
And one thing that
you'll realize

00:32:38.560 --> 00:32:41.260
is that this is on HTTP
not HTTPS because I

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:42.430
was too lazy to set it up.

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:45.130
Sorry.

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:48.250
If I come here, then it's
the same kind of thing.

00:32:48.250 --> 00:32:49.450
From Europe, I go to Europe.

00:32:49.450 --> 00:32:51.740
From US, I go to US.

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:54.160
And if you look at the
configuration, first

00:32:54.160 --> 00:33:02.030
of all, the Kubernetes
Engine, I have two clusters.

00:33:02.030 --> 00:33:05.650
One is where-am-I-Europe-West
and the other one is where

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:06.310
am-I-US-West.

00:33:06.310 --> 00:33:08.510
So these are my
Kubernetes clusters.

00:33:08.510 --> 00:33:11.190
And if you look
at the services, I

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:14.040
have the ingress services and
the NodePort services that

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:16.360
are also exposed that I need.

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:18.420
And if you look at
the look balancers--

00:33:20.990 --> 00:33:25.420
So this was the look
balancer for Compute Engine,

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:29.760
but I also have a looked
balancer for the kubemci.

00:33:29.760 --> 00:33:33.100
And that's pointing to the
ingresses, the backend services

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:35.700
basically in Kubernetes.

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:39.090
So that's how it is.

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:42.660
And so that's Kubernetes
without Istio.

00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:45.620
But if you are running many
microservices on Kubernetes,

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:48.530
and if you want to manage the
traffic to your microservices,

00:33:48.530 --> 00:33:51.710
then it makes
sense to use Istio.

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:57.420
And normally Istio runs
in a single region.

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:00.110
For example, there's this
book info application

00:34:00.110 --> 00:34:02.150
that you probably
have seen on Istio.

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:05.590
It's a sample application that
has multiple microservices.

00:34:05.590 --> 00:34:08.159
And usually these are
deployed in a single region.

00:34:08.159 --> 00:34:12.320
But there is a way to set
Istio in a multi-cluster way

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:14.550
where it can span
multiple clusters.

00:34:14.550 --> 00:34:17.159
And these clusters can
be in multiple regions.

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:20.739
So in this case, for example, we
have one of the microservices.

00:34:20.739 --> 00:34:24.590
We used V2 running
in the US West,

00:34:24.590 --> 00:34:27.050
and then other one
running in US East.

00:34:27.050 --> 00:34:30.920
So you can do this kind of
setup in multi-cluster Istio.

00:34:30.920 --> 00:34:31.590
I tried this.

00:34:31.590 --> 00:34:32.969
It works.

00:34:32.969 --> 00:34:35.420
And if you go to Istio
documentation page,

00:34:35.420 --> 00:34:37.070
there's a couple of
different flavors

00:34:37.070 --> 00:34:39.440
that you can use to set this up.

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:41.030
But we won't go
through it here today

00:34:41.030 --> 00:34:44.330
because it's a little bit
complicated and too many steps.

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:46.320
But it's something
that actually works,

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:49.429
and then you can use as well.

00:34:49.429 --> 00:34:52.489
OK, so that wraps up the
Kubernetes/Istio part.

00:34:52.489 --> 00:34:55.199
And lets talk about Cloud
Run, and App Engine,

00:34:55.199 --> 00:34:56.380
and Cloud Functions land.

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:58.410
JAMES WARD: Yep,
moving up the stack.

00:34:58.410 --> 00:35:01.340
So if you want a higher
abstraction level,

00:35:01.340 --> 00:35:03.940
instead of doing
GCE or Kubernetes,

00:35:03.940 --> 00:35:05.920
you want to move up to
Cloud Run or App Engine.

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:08.460
Let's talk about how that works.

00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:10.820
So for our serverless
compute, there's

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:13.880
different abstraction layers,
Cloud Functions, App Engine,

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:15.110
and Cloud Run.

00:35:15.110 --> 00:35:19.010
And the way that you set up
these to be multi-regional

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:20.030
varies a little bit.

00:35:20.030 --> 00:35:21.350
So I want to run through that.

00:35:21.350 --> 00:35:24.020
So if you're on App
Engine, when you

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:27.350
create an app in
Google Cloud, it's

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:29.190
tied to a specific project.

00:35:29.190 --> 00:35:32.720
And you can only have one
app, one App Engine app,

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:34.640
in a given project.

00:35:34.640 --> 00:35:37.620
And each app has
a single region.

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:39.860
So in App Engine,
there's this correlation

00:35:39.860 --> 00:35:42.320
of one project
has one app, which

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:44.432
is tied to a single region.

00:35:44.432 --> 00:35:45.890
So if you want to
be multi-regional

00:35:45.890 --> 00:35:47.515
with App Engine, what
that means is you

00:35:47.515 --> 00:35:51.920
need to set up multiple projects
and one project for each region

00:35:51.920 --> 00:35:53.668
that you want to be in.

00:35:53.668 --> 00:35:55.460
So that's what we have
to do for App Engine

00:35:55.460 --> 00:35:58.340
to be multi-regional.

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:01.940
For Cloud Functions, we
can have multiple functions

00:36:01.940 --> 00:36:03.330
in a given project.

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:04.340
So that's good.

00:36:04.340 --> 00:36:08.090
But each actual
function in our project

00:36:08.090 --> 00:36:10.010
is tied to a specific region.

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:13.160
So if we want to have a
multi-original function,

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:15.800
then we need to have multiple
instances of that function,

00:36:15.800 --> 00:36:19.830
multiple functions essentially,
in different regions.

00:36:19.830 --> 00:36:23.000
And then for a Cloud Run,
that will also be true.

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:25.040
But today, Cloud Run
I think only works

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:26.840
in the central region.

00:36:26.840 --> 00:36:29.350
So today, only one
region for Cloud Run.

00:36:29.350 --> 00:36:31.070
So we can't quite be
multi-regional yet.

00:36:31.070 --> 00:36:32.720
I'm sure that's coming.

00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:35.120
But when we do have
multiple regions,

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:36.650
it's very similar
to Cloud Function.

00:36:36.650 --> 00:36:40.460
So each time we
create a deployment

00:36:40.460 --> 00:36:44.690
in Cloud Run,
essentially our app, then

00:36:44.690 --> 00:36:46.850
that runs in a
specific region, so we

00:36:46.850 --> 00:36:49.070
need to have multiple ones.

00:36:49.070 --> 00:36:52.340
But at least we can have
multiple Cloud Run apps

00:36:52.340 --> 00:36:54.740
or project--

00:36:54.740 --> 00:36:58.610
deployments within
a given project.

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:01.100
Now for all of these
options today, you

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:03.260
have to use an
external load balancer.

00:37:03.260 --> 00:37:06.770
So Apigee is a great
option because that's

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:09.290
something that is part
of the Google family,

00:37:09.290 --> 00:37:11.420
just not fully integrated
into the Google

00:37:11.420 --> 00:37:14.000
Console and that kind of
thing yet, but great option.

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:17.987
Or you could use another
load balancer as well.

00:37:17.987 --> 00:37:20.570
So those are your options around
the serverless side of things

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:21.960
for how you go multi-regional.

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:23.570
METE ATAMEL: One
thing to add here,

00:37:23.570 --> 00:37:26.870
but we are working on a more
Google Cloud-native solution,

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:29.000
load balancing solution,
for all of these.

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:30.680
It's not there yet,
but it's something

00:37:30.680 --> 00:37:32.220
that we are working on.

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:34.845
But for now, we have to rely on
something external like Apigee,

00:37:34.845 --> 00:37:37.970
or Cloudflare, or
something like that.

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:41.600
And Apigee-- I don't think
I will do justice to Apigee

00:37:41.600 --> 00:37:44.240
because it's such a
complicated product,

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:46.310
but I only use a
small part of that.

00:37:46.310 --> 00:37:48.230
But what is Apigee
is that it used

00:37:48.230 --> 00:37:50.645
to be separate
company that Google

00:37:50.645 --> 00:37:52.800
bought two or three years ago.

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:57.030
It's an API proxy, an
API management tool.

00:37:57.030 --> 00:38:02.720
So you can basically set a proxy
that points to either servers

00:38:02.720 --> 00:38:05.670
or points to any endpoint
that's running anywhere,

00:38:05.670 --> 00:38:07.220
not just on Google Cloud.

00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:09.230
And then you can set
very complicated rules

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:10.790
on those endpoints.

00:38:10.790 --> 00:38:13.190
So you can do
pre-flows, which means

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:16.790
when someone hits that
endpoint, you can run some code.

00:38:16.790 --> 00:38:18.950
Then you can
post-flows, which means

00:38:18.950 --> 00:38:21.140
that when someone
comes out of the code,

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:23.450
you can do some
complicated things.

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:25.160
You can load balance
between anything

00:38:25.160 --> 00:38:28.340
really, like any endpoint,
you can load balance.

00:38:28.340 --> 00:38:31.580
You can set pretty much any
kind of rule you want on that.

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:33.830
They have a really
good developer console

00:38:33.830 --> 00:38:36.050
where you can visualize
things and see

00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:37.430
how the traffic is flowing.

00:38:37.430 --> 00:38:39.930
You can even debug your
endpoints from the console

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:41.460
that I'm going to show.

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:43.980
And there's many other
things like machine learning,

00:38:43.980 --> 00:38:46.740
intrusion, detection,
and all sorts of stuff

00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:48.360
that I haven't played with.

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:51.380
But just know that if you need
any kind of API management,

00:38:51.380 --> 00:38:53.160
Apigee's a really great tool.

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:56.400
Now what I use it for is
that if we have App Engine

00:38:56.400 --> 00:38:59.580
applications, and we had
the App Engine endpoints,

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:01.740
all I want it to
do is that I want

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:04.860
to have a single endpoint
where I can load--

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:06.610
I can do geo load balancing.

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:08.460
So if a user is in,
US-West, I want them

00:39:08.460 --> 00:39:09.840
to go to US-West App Engine.

00:39:09.840 --> 00:39:12.090
And if they're in Europe, I
want them to go to Europe.

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:14.250
So I just set that
up with Apigee.

00:39:14.250 --> 00:39:16.128
And it was really easy
and really intuitive.

00:39:16.128 --> 00:39:17.670
So let me just show
you some of that.

00:39:20.380 --> 00:39:22.255
So this is the App
Engine console.

00:39:22.255 --> 00:39:23.380
So it's a separate console.

00:39:23.380 --> 00:39:25.360
It's not part of Google Cloud.

00:39:25.360 --> 00:39:26.620
So you have to work with that.

00:39:26.620 --> 00:39:29.680
And you can set many proxies
that you want, API proxies.

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:31.892
But here, I have a proxy
for my multi-regional app.

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:39.037
And then what you can see is
that there's prod and test,

00:39:39.037 --> 00:39:41.370
so you don't have to push
everything to prod by default.

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:43.410
You can test them out
in a test environment.

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:45.870
And once you are ready,
you can push them to prod.

00:39:45.870 --> 00:39:48.660
And then under here, you can
see the endpoints that we are

00:39:48.660 --> 00:39:53.180
exposing, so multiregion-atamel
is my base endpoint,

00:39:53.180 --> 00:39:57.300
but then /gae is my
App Engine endpoint,

00:39:57.300 --> 00:40:00.610
/CloudRun is my Cloud Run
endpoint, and stuff like that.

00:40:00.610 --> 00:40:03.180
So these are what's
called proxy endpoints.

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:04.750
I have a default one.

00:40:04.750 --> 00:40:07.410
And I also have specific
ones for specific products,

00:40:07.410 --> 00:40:11.010
like the App Engine, or Cloud
Run, or Cloud Functions.

00:40:11.010 --> 00:40:14.790
And then these proxy endpoints,
they point to target endpoints.

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:16.700
And these target
endpoints, basically

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:18.390
they are things that you define.

00:40:18.390 --> 00:40:20.850
They can be some
endpoints in Google Cloud,

00:40:20.850 --> 00:40:23.430
or they can be endpoints
anywhere, really.

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:26.680
The default one-- and by the
way, this is the UI part,

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:29.300
but you can also switch
to developer mode.

00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:32.280
And in developer mode,
you can see things

00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:37.320
in a way that makes more
sense, at least to me, in XML.

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:38.785
So, for example,
the default one,

00:40:38.785 --> 00:40:40.410
I'm saying that it's
a secure endpoint,

00:40:40.410 --> 00:40:42.630
meaning I'm going to use HTTPS.

00:40:42.630 --> 00:40:46.152
And my target endpoint
is default as well.

00:40:46.152 --> 00:40:47.610
If you look at the
target endpoint,

00:40:47.610 --> 00:40:49.290
I have tow servers defined.

00:40:49.290 --> 00:40:51.120
And I'm just run
[INAUDIBLE] between them.

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:53.370
So these servers I
define what their IP is,

00:40:53.370 --> 00:40:56.890
and what their port is, and
it will [INAUDIBLE] in them.

00:40:56.890 --> 00:41:00.780
If you look at the
App Engine one,

00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:05.790
here I'm saying my
base endpoint is /gae.

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:08.070
And it's also a secure endpoint.

00:41:08.070 --> 00:41:11.280
And then my target endpoint
is GAE Europe West and GAE

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:13.170
US West 2.

00:41:13.170 --> 00:41:14.460
And I also have a condition.

00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:16.380
And in this
condition, I'm saying

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:20.340
that if the system has a region
name that starts with Europe,

00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:21.840
then send it to Europe West.

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:23.910
And if the system region
name starts with US,

00:41:23.910 --> 00:41:24.750
send it to US West.

00:41:24.750 --> 00:41:28.560
So that's how I'm doing a geo
load balancing with Apigee.

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:33.970
And if you look at these target
endpoints, GAE Europe West,

00:41:33.970 --> 00:41:36.910
it's basically just my
App Engine and a URL.

00:41:36.910 --> 00:41:39.880
So this is a
multi-region in Europe.

00:41:39.880 --> 00:41:41.860
And this is multi-region
2, which is in the US.

00:41:41.860 --> 00:41:43.340
So that's how you set it up.

00:41:43.340 --> 00:41:49.040
And once you have that, you can
also do tracing kind of things.

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:49.855
But maybe I won't--

00:41:49.855 --> 00:41:51.730
yeah, I won't show that
